+### [Volumetric Inverse Rendering via Neural Radiative Transfer](10840-volumetric-inverse-rendering-via-neural-radiative-transfer.md)
+Performs volumetric inverse rendering by jointly optimizing a neural medium field and a neural light field. It uses a Volumetric Radiance Error (VRE) loss to integrate radiance along primary viewing rays, counteracting oversmoothing from local constraints, and amortizes global light transport resolution.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by ray tracing for primal rendering and differential queries, plus network inference. Avoids storing large computation graphs compared to end-to-end differentiable rendering.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low compared to path-tracing-based inverse rendering, as gradients are cached in the neural network rather than computed via backpropagation through every light path.
-*   **Strengths:** Scalable to complex scenes; decouples rendering from optimization; avoids memory explosion of automatic differentiation.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-training of primal radiance; bias introduced if networks are not fine-tuned; sensitivity to initialization of $L_\phi$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** The method avoids the high cost of differentiating through generic Monte Carlo rendering (as in RB) by using an adjoint-like approach with a cached neural representation. However, fine-tuning the networks at every iteration is a bottleneck [S1, unofficial]. Using the RHS approximation reduces this cost but introduces bias [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing two neural networks (primal and differential) and sparse feature grids. The computation graph size is vastly reduced compared to generic differentiable path tracing [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** The method is efficient for scenes where global light transport is complex, as it amortizes the transport solution. It is suitable for optimizing material properties (e.g., roughness) and illumination [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Avoids independent light transport simulations per scene instance; handles global illumination via the neural network; reduces computation graph size [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Fine-tuning bottleneck if not using the RHS approximation; potential bias from using the RHS approximation; requires pre-training of the primal radiance network [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the work of Hadadan et al. (2021/2022), extending Neural Radiosity [Hadadan et al. 2021] to the inverse domain. It addresses the computational cost and memory limitations of standard differentiable rendering by using a neural network to approximate the differential radiance field, allowing for efficient gradient computation via the differential rendering equation [S1, unofficial].
+The method is based on "Differentiable Neural Radiosity" by Hadadan et al. (2022) [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Neural Radiosity method by Hadadan et al. (2021) [S1, unofficial]. The approach was developed to address the computational costs of differentiable rendering by caching the differential radiance distribution in a neural network [S1, unofficial].
 
